{
 "_comment": "What a name model often mistakes for personal data in Indonesian text. `organisations`: public banks, payment apps, couriers, platforms and government bodies, never masked when the name model reports them (patterns and your own terms still mask). `not_names`: forms of address, pronouns, roles and generic institution words, never a person or an organisation on their own. `public_prefixes`: an organisation the name model reports that starts with one of these words (kementerian, pengadilan, partai...) is a public body. `places`: Indonesian cities and regencies, recognised as the end of an address wherever they appear. `titles`: taken off the front of a person's name, so 'Bapak Budi' masks as 'Bapak [PERSON_1]'. Turn the organisations off with find(..., public=False). `facilities`: words that start a health facility's name (RS, RSUD, Klinik, Apotek...): such an organisation is public, and a person's name inside one (RSUD Dr. Soetomo) is a namesake, not a person. `facility_suffixes`: words after a name that make it a facility (Hermina Hospital, Bunda Medika). `phrase_breaks`: linking words that end a facility's name ('RS Hermina oleh dr. Budi'). `disease_before`, `disease_after`: a disease named after a person (sindrom Down, Crohn disease). Not 'penyakit' or 'anemia': 'penyakit Budi' is Budi's illness.",
